  "implementation_text": "Promoted grouping is `ratio_matched_neuron`. It requires 48 matched TIA arrays (one per neuron) in which Rf shares the same resistor family as that neuron's weights and bias. Invert R1/R2 and divider Ra/Rb must be pair-matched. Polarity pairs should be co-located; a chip-wide +/\u2212 field is destructive. Common-centroid / interdigitated placement is the abstraction that reduces linear-gradient residual. This is a matching *relationship*, not a claimed die area.",
  "count_text": "Current compiled fabric: 1342 logical resistors (1024 weight, 48 bias, 48 Rf, 98 invert, 124 divider). Blind N-tiling of all of them was already rejected by Experiment 27 (N=16 still ~2.6e-3 on o2). Ratio matching does not require multiplying every resistor; it requires grouping the TIA arrays. At G_unit=G_W/16 the TIA fabric becomes 36447 unit elements vs 1342 logical resistors (27.16\u00d7) and still quantization-fails (MAE 5.967e-02). Quantization-only analog-pass first occurs at G_unit=7.324e-09 (9328997 units, ~6952\u00d7). Area proxy = element count, not mm\u00b2.",
  "failures_text": "Failed or insufficient: independent mean MAE 4.799e-03; global_plus_local mean MAE 4.383e-03; rf_independent mean MAE 3.820e-03; pn_pair mean MAE 2.815e-03; pn_pair_plus_rf mean MAE 3.718e-03; pn_systematic mean MAE 1.104e-02; centroid_stat mean MAE 1.930e-03. Independent absolute 0.5% fails. +/\u2212 pairing without Rf\u00b7G tracking fails or is weak (Experiment 27 lesson, confirmed). Naive polarity-split placement makes a linear gradient look like systematic polarity mismatch. Blind tiling is not promoted.",
  "final_status": "RESISTOR-RATIO MATCHING PARTIALLY VALIDATED",
  "next_experiment": "Experiment 30 should not rebuild the encoding and should not transistorize Network F. The remaining physical gaps are a documented PDK (resistor TCR, MOS/resistor mismatch PDF, real unit-array geometry) and replacing the Architecture A invert VCVS. Independent 0.5% remains unacceptable. Do not invent foundry coefficients. Do not retrain.",
